{
  "requirement": "An item with no attachments has an empty list. A language may return empty\nlists for every item.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has two parts. An item with no attachments has an empty list. A language may return empty lists for every item, which is permitted and imposes nothing to check.\n\n`crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs#fn[bundled_languages_classify_only_their_attachment_syntax]` is marked `{test}` for `requirements.sdk.outlines.attachments.empty`. It covers the requirement in two ways:\n- **Plain text:** The `test.txt` case (`\"# just text\\n\"`) expects `vec![]`. I checked `plain_text` in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/mod.rs`. It creates a root item and one `line` item per non-blank line, so the outline does have items. The test flattens every item's `attachments` and asserts the result is empty. That shows each of those items has an empty list, and the text `# just text` looks like a comment but is not classified as one. The check is not vacuous.\n- **Other languages:** Every other case compares the flattened attachments of all items against an exact expected list. An item that should have none but received a spurious attachment would add an unexpected entry and fail the `assert_eq!`. The `def[f]` assertion also checks the exact attachment count.\n\nThe only plausible violation is an item without attachments that gets a non-empty list. The exact-match assertions in the test would catch it. The requirement adds no further boundary conditions. I found no gaps.",
